    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193547": {
      "title": "Clinical Associate Professor - Head & Neck Anatomy",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: University of Rochester Medical Center: School of Medicine and Dentistry: Dentistry Location: School of Medicine & Dentistry, Eastman Institute for Oral Health The Eastman Institute for Oral Health at the University of Rochester School of Medicine and Dentistry seeks a faculty member at the rank of Clinical Associate Professor for periodic didactic teaching of Head & Neck Anatomy. This is a non-clinical appointment. Key responsibilities may include: Didactic Teaching: Serve as the primary instructor for DEN410 Head & Neck Anatomy, delivering lectures, coordinating course materials, and evaluating student performance during scheduled alternating years. Curriculum Development: Update and refine course content for DEN410 to reflect current anatomical knowledge and clinical relevance for dental and oral health residents or graduate students. Student Assessment: Design and grade examinations, practical assessments, and assignments associated with the anatomy curriculum. Academic Service: Participate in departmental meetings, curriculum committees, and academic mentoring as assigned by the EIOH leadership. Education: MD, Ph.D. in Anatomy, or equivalent terminal degree from an accredited institution. Experience: Demonstrated expertise in teaching gross anatomy, specifically head and neck structures, at the graduate, dental, or medical school level. If you already have an Interfolio account, please sign in to apply to this position. If not, please create an Interfolio account. The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum annual salary for this faculty position. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range, and will be determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations. Incentive-based compensation may also be applicable and will be discussed during the hiring process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/176297": {
      "title": "Senior Tenured Faculty-High Energy Density (HED) science",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Hajim School of Engineering & Applied Sciences: Dean's Office SEAS Location: Lattimore Hall The Hajim School of Engineering and Applied Sciences and the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Rochester, invite applications for a senior tenured faculty position in the field of High Energy Density (HED) science. The successful candidate will be a distinguished scholar with a world-class research program, in the school(s) and department(s) best suiting the candidate. We are looking for a dynamic individual to join our faculty and lead efforts in this rapidly evolving field, with a planned start date of Fall Semester 2026. The University of Rochester is a private, comprehensive institution dedicated to student success, academic excellence, and cutting-edge research. The Hajim School of Engineering and Applied Sciences as well as the School of Arts and Sciences and their implicit departments are collaborative, world leading academic units committed to high-quality teaching and breakthrough research. The University is home to the Laboratory for Laser Energetics which houses the largest university-based lasers together with ~300 scientists focused on Fusion, high energy density science, plasmas, and lasers.  The Center for Matter at Atomic Pressures, an NSF Physics Frontier Center focused on understanding the nature of extreme matter and its implications to planets, stars, and revolutionary states of matter, is also based at the University of Rochester. High Energy Density (HED) Science HED science is a rapidly evolving, interdisciplinary research frontier that focuses on understanding and controlling matter under extreme conditions, such as those found inside stars or the cores of giant planets. The field has critical applications for society, including fusion energy, planetary science, and national security. We are seeking a candidate with a strong research background in one or more areas of HED science, which may include: Astrophysics and Planetary Science under extreme conditions Inertial Confinement Fusion (ICF) and Fusion Energy Warm Dense Matter Dynamic Materials under extreme pressure Laser-Plasma Interactions Nuclear and Atomic Physics in Plasma Environments Extreme Quantum Matter Plasma Physics There is excellent collaboration among faculty across multiple departments, and close ties to the Laboratory for Laser Energetics and the Goergen Institute for Data Science and Artificial Intelligence. The city of Rochester, NY is a diverse, energetic, and family-friendly community located on Lake Ontario adjacent to the Finger Lakes region with a metropolitan population of 1+ million. Rochester also boasts a thriving arts and music culture, substantial employment opportunities in various sectors, affordable housing, a rich history of social justice, and easy access to urban, suburban, and rural neighborhoods Questions concerning this position can be addressed to Gilbert Collins ( gcollins@lle@rochester.edu , chair of the search committee and Tracy Hyde Harris, Professor in Mechanical Engineering and in Physics. Review of applications will begin on April 1st, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Salary: $180K - $230K (9-month salary) The referenced pay range represents the full base range of pay for this job. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on market data, experience, and expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations. The University of Rochester community is committed to Meliora – becoming ever better. Through our Vision & Values statement ( https://www.rochester.edu/aboutus/values/ ) we affirm our commitment to equity, leadership, integrity, openness, respect and accountability. EOE / Minorities / Females / Protected Veterans / Disabled Candidates should hold a PhD degree (or equivalent) in Physics, Applied Physics, Astronomy, Engineering, or a closely related field and should have considerable research experience. Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter, 2) a CV including a list of publications, 3) a statement of research, 4) a teaching statement, 5) OPTIONAL: a statement discussing the ways in which your experiences will shape your pursuits as a member of our faculty and help you add to the University's core values of Meliora (ever better), 6) up to three articles (as three separate attachments), and 7) contact information for three referees who we may contact at later stages of the interview process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/142478": {
      "title": "Group Leader for AI/ML Group (Scientist)",
      "description": "Posted: Mar 06, 2024 Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Rochester, NY The Theoretical Division at the University of Rochester's Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) is planning to create an artificial intelligence/machine-learning (AI/ML) Group to significantly expand LLE's effort in applying ML tools to solve a variety of problems relevant to high-energy-density (HED) sciences, operate current laser facilities (OMEGA and OMEGA EP), as well as design and operate future HED facilities that will deliver ~100 of shots a day and will require sophisticated tools for facility operation, engineering, and data acquisition and analysis. LLE seeks an AI/ML expert to lead this group. The ideal candidate for this role should have a strong background in machine learning with focus on physical science and technology, with experience in leading teams and developing solutions that provide measurable outcomes. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in physics, astrophysics, computational physics, or a related field. Candidate should submit a cover letter a curriculum vitae and a list of publications. at least three letters of recommendation a statement of research interests and accomplishments, a vision statement for the AI/ML group's future; and SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/142690": {
      "title": "Senior Mechanical Design Engineer",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Rochester, NY We are in search of a Mechanical Design Engineer with a strong background in design engineering. The candidate will provide engineering solutions to a diverse range of complex technical problems throughout LLE.  The ideal candidate will have in-depth knowledge of machine component design, materials, knowledge of manufacturing processes and experience with complex mechanical systems.  Experience in opto-mechanics, precision mechanics, tool and fixture design, high vacuum systems, heat transfer, cryogenic design principles, motion control, and/or precision metrology is desired. Key Responsibilities: Develop conceptual and detailed designs of mechanical parts and assemblies based on project requirements. Provide supporting safety and engineering analyses as required. Prepare materials for engineering design reviews. Prepare technical drawings in compliance with industry standards. Work both independently and as a part of a multidisciplinary team. BSME with 8 yrs experience or graduate engineering degree with 6 years industry experience. Proficient with CAD software, Siemens NX preferred. Proficient with FEA software,  ANSYS preferred. Competent with enterprise class PDM or PLM software, Siemens Teamcenter preferred Proficient in documentation Strong verbal and written communication skills to effectively communicate research findings and project progress to internal and external stakeholders within the Siemens ecosystem. Please upload a resume and cover letter SALARY RANGE: $90,000 to $175,000 annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/163654": {
      "title": "AI/ML Group Leader (Research Engineer)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The Theoretical Division at the University of Rochester's Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE)has recently formed an artificial intelligence/machine-learning (AI/ML) Group to significantly expand LLE's effort in applying ML tools to solve a variety of problems relevant to high-energy-density (HED) sciences, operate current laser facilities (OMEGA and OMEGA EP), as well as design and operate future HED facilities that will deliver ~100 of shots a day and will require sophisticated tools for facility operation, engineering, and data acquisition and analysis. LLE seeks an AI/ML expert to lead this group. The ideal candidate for this role should have a strong background in machine learning with focus on physical science and technology, with experience in leading teams and developing solutions that provide measurable outcomes. Candidates should have a MS or Ph.D. in physics, astrophysics, computational physics, or a related field. physics, astrophysics, computational physics, or a related field. Candidate should submit a cover letter a curriculum vitae and a list of publications. at least two letters of recommendation a statement of research interests and accomplishments, a vision statement for the AI/ML group's future; and SALARY RANGE: $90,000-$175,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181032": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The URochester has exciting opportunities for scientists to join the Laser-Plasma Interactions Group in the PULSE Division at LLE, while collaborating directly with plasma scientists at the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory—potentially stationed at LLNL. With the success of inertial confinement fusion, the community is evaluating next generation facilities to support the stockpile stewardship mission. One of LLE’s roles is to push the current understanding through simulations of the effects of laser-bandwidth on laser-plasma instabilities relevant to laser indirect drive and direct-drive fusion in future experiments aimed at delivering GJ-class yields. A key to inertial fusion is the mitigation of laser-plasma instabilities, and the team is developing the scientific understanding of how laser bandwidth can be applied to reach the goal of robust ignition through both experiments and modeling.  In 2026, the Fourth generation Laser for Ultrabroadband eXperiments (FLUX) will begin experiments on the OMEGA Facility to demonstrate the mitigation of LPIs using laser bandwidth. These focused experiments will test our understanding of how laser bandwidth mitigates single-beam LPI (e.g., filamentation, stimulated Brillouin or Raman scattering) and multi-beam instabilities like cross-beam energy transfer and two-plasmon decay instability. Modeling to support these experiments will use the codes pF3D, LPSE (Laser Plasma Simulation Environment), OSIRIS, and WARP-X, which are the foundation of our team’s development of reduced LPI models that are being implemented in radiation-hydrodynamics codes. The successful candidate will join an excellent team of accomplished experimental and computational scientists. The candidate will have direct responsibility to support the development of our understanding of the effects of bandwidth on laser-plasma instabilities relevant to future fusion experiments. Candidates will be expected to actively pursue publications in peer reviewed journals and present their work at national and international conferences. Ph.D. Up to 10 years experience a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195087": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist (Theoretical Studies of Laser-Material Interactions)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics Job Overview: Develop modeling capabilities to investigated the interaction of laser pulses with nanostructures and nanofeatures. Investigate the origin of laser induced damage in complex optical materials (gratings, multilayer dielectric coatings, meta optics, mixed materials) . Help develop designing criteria for next generation optical coatings with extended lifetime performance. Active communication/collaboration with other internal and external groups with targeted scope of work that is relevant to LLE’s current and future needs. Responsibilities and Duties: Work closely with scientist and engineers with expertise in optical design, coating fabrication, material characterization and laser damage testing. Interface with LLE staff while collaborating on projects and supporting facility operations. Communicate research results through internal reports, publish in refereed journals, and present at internal laboratory meetings and conference settings. Survey, study, and peer review relevant scientific literature Responsible for developing and maintaining computational capabilities in support of optical materials development and fabrication of coating and laser components PhD – Physics, Applied Physics, Materials Science, or other relevant fields Experience using multiphysics computational tools . Strong background in electromagnetic theory and applications. Research experience in the interaction of high intensity electromagnetic waves with material (propagation, energy coupling, nonlinear processes etc.) The applicant should submit: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least three letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $82,500 to $110,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185021": {
      "title": "Research Engineer",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics Position Summary: The Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) is seeking a highly skilled and collaborative Lead AI/ML Engineer to architect, implement, and scale applied machine learning solutions that support LLE’s mission in inertial confinement fusion (ICF), high-energy-density physics, laser diagnostics, and materials science. This role focuses on engineering leadership, ML infrastructure development, and hands-on deployment of advanced models including large language models (LLMs), agentic AI systems, and scientific inference pipelines. The successful candidate will work closely with a Principal Investigator (PI) leading ML research efforts, as well as with physicists, chemists, and experimental teams, to operationalize AI/ML models for real-time control, simulation analysis, materials discovery, and data visualization. This is a high-impact technical role for an experienced engineer with a passion for interdisciplinary science. Key Responsibilities: Design and deploy ML systems to support scientific workflows, including model training, serving, and lifecycle management. Develop and integrate LLMs, agentic AI tools, and decision-support systems to augment scientific reasoning and diagnostics. Collaborate with research staff to productionize ML models for: Laser and plasma system modeling Experimental diagnostics and control Materials property prediction and DFT acceleration Define and maintain infrastructure for AI workloads: GPU/accelerator provisioning Containerized deployment (Docker, Kubernetes/OpenShift) Model versioning and reproducibility (e.g., MLflow, DVC) Lead internal initiatives for AI/ML training, best practices, and developer support across scientific groups. Contribute to software engineering decisions, including specification of new hardware, resource planning, and technical roadmapping. Collaborate with academic and industrial partners to evaluate and adopt emerging AI technologies. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Computer Science, Engineering, AI/ML, or a related technical field (PhD optional). Experience in machine learning engineering, with strong emphasis on: Deep learning frameworks (e.g., PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX) ML infrastructure and model deployment Large language models (e.g., OpenAI, Hugging Face, open-source stacks like LLaMA or Mistral) Experience building scalable ML pipelines and integrating models with production systems. Proficiency in modern software engineering practices: version control, CI/CD, containerization, and API development. Strong communication and organizational skills, with the ability to support scientists in diverse domains. Preferred Qualifications: Experience deploying AI systems in a research, scientific computing, or HPC environment. Familiarity with agent-based reasoning systems and tools like LangChain, OpenAgents, or custom tool-use frameworks. Understanding of physical science applications such as ICF, materials design, or experimental diagnostics. Experience contributing to cross-functional teams spanning research and engineering. Interest in mentoring junior engineers or interns, and developing internal training tools or documentation. The applicant should submit: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $90,000 to $175,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185017": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY: The Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (AI/ML) Group in the Theory Division of the LLE is seeking an outstanding research scientist to advance AI/ML initiatives across the inertial confinement fusion (ICF) program. This role integrates rigorous scientific investigation and theoretical insight with expertise in AI/ML methods, translating these capabilities into practical impact in process optimization, diagnostic data analysis, agentic AI development, and data management. The successful candidate will make significant contributions to on-going efforts in optimization of material system fabrication processes, LLM-orchestrated agentic system development, and support efforts in physics-informed and PDE-based AI/ML code development. Candidates must have strong experience in scientific AI/ML methods, differentiable programming, and experimental modeling, as well as solid programming skills in Python, PyTorch, JAX, or other AI/ML and differentiable programming languages. The ideal candidate will have expertise in one or more of the following areas: computational science and engineering, computer science, and materials science and process optimization. RESPONSIBILITIES: Take a leading role in designing AI/ML frameworks to aid in process optimization and fabrication of high-quality optical materials and devices for high-power, high-energy laser systems Oversee the development of a secure, internal RAG-based chat service and scientific knowledge retention/recommendation system Contribute to the development and uncertainty estimation of AI emulators for physics-based codes used in ICF research Develop inverse design and real-time optimization capabilities using INNs, GNNs, neural operators, or other appropriate methods Collaborate with experimentalists and engineering staff to deploy scalable and real-time ML tools Publish research results in high-impact, peer-reviewed journals Serve as principal investigator or co-investigator on Genesis Mission and other research proposals Advise graduate and undergraduate students conducting AI/ML research MINIMUM EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Ph D in machine learning, physics, computational physics, computer science, applied mathematics, or engineering Demonstrated proficiency in scientific computing with one or more of: Python, PyTorch, Julia, Jax, C++, Java or other modern computing languages At least five years of research experience in computational sciences, including a minimum of two years of postdoctoral experience at national laboratories or universities Strong written and oral communication skills Ability to work effectively, both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team The applicant should submit: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185185": {
      "title": "Division Director - Laser & Materials Technology",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics Overview: The Laser and Materials Technology (LMT) Division presently consists of five groups including Laser Technology Development (LTD), Optical Materials Technology (OMT), Optical Manufacturing (OMAN), Laser System Projects (LSP), and MTW/MTW-OPAL Laser Facility Operations. The over 100 division members comprises a multidisciplinary of over 60 division members that develop and maintain technology, custom equipment, and the infrastructure needed to conduct research at LLE. The LMT Division Director will direct these groups to align activities with laboratory goals and will report to the LLE Director. The Division leader will serve as part of the senior management team of LLE. Responsibilities: Direct assigned resources to conduct research, development and production of novel laser, optics, optical materials, and diagnostic technologies for LLE and other research facilities. Direct assigned resources to ensure the safe, effective, and efficient operation of the MTW/MTW-OPAL Laser Facility and the FLUX (Fourth-generation Laser for Ultrabroadband eXperiments). Working with other Division leaders and the Laboratory Director, develop the annual budget formulation process of LLE INST 8100 for the LMT Division to ensure resources, human and capital, are sufficient to support Laboratory priorities. Monitor and track performance to ensure that resources are deployed properly and identify and address any issues that may arise.  Propose and oversee appropriate corrective actions. Foster the equitable and efficient management of assigned human resources, including performance and salary review, recognition and advancement, and compliance with all University personnel policies. Within available resources, identify and actively recruit high-quality staff. Ph.D. degree in Optics, Physics, or a related discipline with at least 15 years of engineering experience and a proven record managing multi-disciplinary resources in an operations environment.  Excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work in a team environment are essential. The candidate must also demonstrate a track record of managing the successful completion of both large and small projects. Include a cover letter that addresses why you should be considered for the position of Laser and Materials Technology Division Director and the near-term goals you would pursue in the first year. The applicant should submit: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least four letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $140,000 to $250,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187671": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY: This position is an appointment at the University of Rochester’s Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE). The Integrated Modeling Group in the Theory Division of the LLE is seeking an outstanding computational and theoretical scientist to conduct rigorous, high-impact research in radiation-hydrodynamic modeling and development relevant to inertial confinement fusion (ICF), high energy-density physics, and fundamental science. The successful candidate will make significant contributions to ongoing efforts in high-fidelity modeling of ICF and HED experiments on OMEGA and NIF and associated code validation; support and develop necessary improvements in LLE modeling tools, and work closely with experimental physicists to plan and execute experiments. Candidates must have strong experience in computational physics and experimental modeling, as well as solid programming skills in C++, Fortran, or other modern programming languages. The ideal candidate will have expertise in one or more of the following areas: computational physics, radiation hydrodynamics, and plasma physics. RESPONSIBILITIES: Take a leading role in designing and modeling radiation hydrodynamic modeling ICF and HED experiments conducted on the OMEGA and NIF laser systems Contribute to the development of new physics capabilities for LLE’s radiation-hydrodynamics codes Apply modern high-performance computational techniques to improve the performance of radiation-hydrodynamics codes at LLE Collaborate with experimentalists at LLE and partner institutions for validation of LLE codes against experimental data Publish research results in high-impact, peer-reviewed journals Advise graduate and undergraduate students conducting computational ICF research Ph D in physics, computational physics, astrophysics, HED physics, or plasma physics Demonstrated proficiency in scientific computing with one or more of: C++, Fortran 90, or other modern computing languages At least five years of research experience in computational sciences, including a minimum of two years of postdoctoral experience at national laboratories or universities Strong written and oral communication skills Ability to work effectively, both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188032": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY The Experimental Division is seeking a Scientist with expertise in shock physics. A successful candidate will have a strong knowledge of dynamic compression experiments with experience designing and conducting experiments using gas gun or laser drivers. RESPONSIBLITIES : Design, build, and trouble-shoot experimental platforms to measure the equation of state, structural changes, and deformation and fracture of materials. Support the operation and improvement of existing and newly acquired equipment and instrumentation for laser physics experiments. Work in an environment with a visible and audible laser operating at both single shot and constant 10 Hz rep rates. QUALIFICATIONS: PhD in Physics, Mechanical Engineering, or related field US Citizenship Ability to obtain a security clearance ESSENTIAL SKILLS: Experience with optical velocity measurements (photon doppler velocimetry [PDV] or velocity interferometer for any reflector [VISAR]) in dynamic compression experiments. Proficiency in Microsoft Office and ability to learn additional software operation Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. and navigate stairs/ladders Excellent verbal and written communication Work both independently and as a part of a multidisciplinary team PREFERRED SKILLS Experience with programming language such as Python or C++ Experience with shock physics material codes such as Hyades or CTH Experience with x-ray or optical spectroscopy Technical writing experience and documentation skills Attention to detail a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE:  $82,500-$110,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189186": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The University of Rochester's Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) seeks an outstanding experimental scientist to join the Relativistic Plasma Physics Experiments (RLX) Group within the Plasma and Ultrafast Laser Science and Engineering (PULSE) Division. The successful candidate will develop and lead a research program focused on the interaction of high-intensity, short-pulse lasers with matter and the creation, diagnosis, and application of relativistic plasmas. This position offers the opportunity to conduct forefront experiments on the OMEGA EP, MTW, and MTW-OPAL laser systems while collaborating with scientists across LLE's plasma physics, high-energy-density science, inertial fusion energy, and ultrafast laser programs. Research Areas The successful candidate will be expected to establish an internationally recognized research program in one or more of the following areas: Short-pulse laser–matter interactions, including laser energy deposition, hot-electron generation and transport, refluxing, scattering processes, and hydrodynamic evolution. Development and application of advanced diagnostics to characterize relativistic plasma conditions and energy coupling mechanisms. Bright x-ray source development for high-energy-density science, opacity measurements, laboratory astrophysics, and fusion-energy applications. Fundamental studies of relativistic plasma physics relevant to particle acceleration, radiation generation, and extreme states of matter. Experimental validation of simulation and modeling tools used to predict laser-plasma interaction and energy transport in high-energy-density plasmas. Development of novel experimental platforms leveraging advanced laser technologies, including ultrashort-pulse, broadband, and spatiotemporally structured laser beams. Responsibilities Conceive, develop, and lead innovative experimental research programs. Design and execute experiments on LLE and external user facilities. Publish research results in leading scientific journals and present findings at major international conferences. Develop proposals and secure external funding from federal agencies and other sponsors. Collaborate closely with scientists, engineers, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, and undergraduate students. Mentor early-career researchers and contribute to workforce development within the PULSE Division. Support the strategic growth of LLE's programs in high-energy-density science, inertial fusion energy, and advanced laser-plasma interactions. About the RLX Group and PULSE Division The RLX Group conducts experimental research at the frontiers of relativistic laser-plasma interactions, advanced particle acceleration, radiation generation, and high-energy-density science. As part of the PULSE Division, the group works closely with complementary experimental, theoretical, and diagnostic teams to develop predictive understanding and control of laser-driven plasmas while enabling new scientific capabilities for fusion energy, discovery science, and national security applications. LLE provides access to world-leading experimental facilities including OMEGA, OMEGA EP, MTW, and the emerging MTW-OPAL capability, offering unique opportunities to pursue transformative research across a broad range of plasma conditions. Required: Ph.D. in Physics, Plasma Physics, Nuclear Engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated record of experimental research excellence in high-energy-density physics, laser-plasma interactions, short-pulse laser science, or a related discipline. Strong publication record and evidence of scientific leadership. Preferred: Experience with high-power short-pulse laser systems. Expertise in x-ray diagnostics, opacity experiments, radiation generation, or laboratory astrophysics. Experience developing new experimental platforms and diagnostics. Familiarity with computational modeling of laser-plasma interactions and energy transport. Experience leading collaborative research efforts and mentoring students or postdoctoral researchers. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189368": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) at the University of Rochester invites applications for a Scientist position in the Ultrafast Plasma Photonics (UPP) Group. The UPP Group is pioneering new ways to control intense laser light in space and time to create fundamentally new interactions between light and plasma. By integrating theory, computation, and experiment, the group is establishing the scientific foundations for Ultrafast Plasma Photonics—an emerging field that combines ultrafast optics, nonlinear photonics, plasma physics, and structured light to enable new regimes of laser–matter interaction. Our research seeks to understand and control laser propagation, plasma dynamics, and nonlinear optical processes using advanced concepts such as flying-focus pulses, spatiotemporal beam shaping, structured light, plasma photonic devices, and programmable laser waveforms. These advances are opening new opportunities in high-energy-density science, inertial fusion energy, plasma-based particle acceleration, ultrafast radiation sources, and next-generation high-power laser systems. The successful candidate will conduct innovative theoretical and computational research in close partnership with experimental scientists, developing predictive models that guide experiments, reveal new physical phenomena, and shape future research directions. Responsibilities Develop original theoretical descriptions of ultrafast laser-plasma interactions and nonlinear optical phenomena. Create predictive computational models of structured-light propagation, plasma dynamics, and laser-driven nonlinear processes. Develop advanced simulation capabilities for spatiotemporally structured laser pulses, including flying-focus and related beam-engineering concepts. Investigate fundamental plasma-photon interactions, plasma-based optical elements, and new approaches for controlling intense laser propagation. Work closely with experimental teams to design experiments, interpret measurements, and identify new scientific opportunities. Develop high-performance computational tools and numerical methods for modeling complex laser-plasma systems. Publish research in leading scientific journals and present results at national and international conferences. Contribute to the development of externally funded research programs. Mentor graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and undergraduate researchers. Research Environment LLE provides a uniquely collaborative environment in which theory, computation, laser technology, diagnostics, and experiments are developed together. Scientists in the UPP Group collaborate closely with researchers across the PULSE Division to explore new frontiers in ultrafast laser science and plasma physics while contributing to nationally important research programs. Current research activities include: Ultrafast Plasma Photonics Structured light and spatiotemporal laser control Flying-focus physics Nonlinear wave propagation Laser-plasma interactions Plasma-based particle acceleration Photon acceleration High-energy-density science Inertial fusion energy Advanced radiation sources Researchers have access to world-leading experimental facilities, including OMEGA, OMEGA EP, MTW-OPAL, FLUX, and future high-power laser systems currently under development. About the Laboratory for Laser Energetics The Laboratory for Laser Energetics at the University of Rochester is an internationally recognized center for research in high-energy-density science, inertial fusion, laser-plasma interactions, ultrafast laser science, and advanced laser technologies. Through the close integration of theory, simulation, engineering, and experiment, LLE develops transformative scientific capabilities that advance fundamental discovery while addressing national priorities in energy, security, and advanced technology. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Physics, Applied Physics, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated excellence in theoretical and computational physics. Strong background in one or more of the following areas: Plasma physics Nonlinear optics Ultrafast optics Computational electromagnetics Laser-plasma interactions Experience developing numerical models and scientific software. Strong programming skills using modern scientific computing languages. Demonstrated ability to publish impactful research and work effectively in collaborative research environments. Preferred Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is desirable: Structured light Flying-focus optics Spatiotemporal beam shaping Plasma photonics Laser-plasma interaction physics Particle-in-cell (PIC), Vlasov, fluid, or hybrid plasma simulations Maxwell solvers and computational electromagnetics Plasma-based particle acceleration High-energy-density plasma physics Scientific machine learning GPU computing and high-performance computing a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189660": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY: This position is an appointment at the University of Rochester’s Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE). The Integrated Modeling Group in the Theory Division of the LLE is seeking an outstanding computational and theoretical scientist to conduct rigorous, high-impact research in radiation-hydrodynamic modeling and development relevant to inertial confinement fusion (ICF), high energy-density physics, and fundamental science. The successful candidate will make significant contributions to ongoing efforts in high-fidelity modeling of ICF and HED experiments on OMEGA and NIF and associated code validation; support and develop necessary improvements in LLE modeling tools, and work closely with experimental physicists to plan and execute experiments. Candidates must have strong experience in computational physics and experimental modeling, as well as solid programming skills in C++, Fortran, or other modern programming languages. The ideal candidate will have expertise in one or more of the following areas: computational physics, radiation hydrodynamics, and plasma physics. RESPONSIBILITIES : Design and model radiation hydrodynamic modeling ICF and HED experiments conducted on the OMEGA and NIF laser systems Contribute to the development of new physics capabilities for LLE’s radiation-hydrodynamics codes Collaborate with experimentalists at LLE and partner institutions in the NNSA national-lab complex Publish research results in high-impact, peer-reviewed journals Advise graduate and undergraduate students conducting computational ICF research MINIMUM EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Ph D in physics, computational physics, astrophysics, HED physics, or plasma physics Demonstrated proficiency in scientific computing with one or more of: C++, Fortran 90, or other modern computing languages Strong written and oral communication skills Ability to work effectively, both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $82,500 to $110,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190461": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY The Experimental Division is seeking a Scientist to study, characterized and develop existing and novel optical and laser materials.  A successful candidate will possess expertise in the design, planning, and execution of experiments for studying solid state material properties and their response to different types of optical and mechanical damage. The candidate will be challenged by the wide range of mechanisms at play in laser-material interactions in various classes of materials. RESPONSIBLITIES : Support the study of laser-material interactions. Support long term development of laser gain materials. Design, build, and trouble-shoot experimental systems that include lasers, imaging, and interferometric diagnostics. Support the operation of existing and newly acquired equipment and instrumentation for laser physics experiments. Work in an environment with a visible and audible laser operating at both single shot and constant 10 Hz rep rates. QUALIFICATIONS: PhD with equivalent experience in Optics, Materials Science, Physical Chemistry, or related field Ability to obtain a security clearance ESSENTIAL SKILLS: Experience with optical materials characterization and optical metrology techniques Proficiency in Microsoft Office and ability to learn additional software operation Ability to lift up to 50 lbs and navigate stairs/ladders Excellent verbal and written communication Work both independently and as a part of a multidisciplinary team Attention to detail PREFERRED SKILLS Experience with programming language such as Python or C++ Technical writing experience and documentation skills APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $82,500 to $110X,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191003": {
      "title": "LLE ME Group Leader (Research Engineer)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The Mechanical Engineering (ME) group is responsible for providing support for the world class Omega Laser facility and scientists who are on the forefront of fusion, high energy density physics, and materials research. The ME Group Leader (MEGL) oversees a group of ~40 mechanical engineers, mechanical specialists, instrument model makers, and students that are vertically integrated to execute the design, fabrication, assembly, installation, and integration of instruments and equipment into the Omega facilities and support laboratories. The MEGL’s direct reports consists of three managers and a team of senior engineers. The MEGL facilitates the timely execution of projects monitoring the workflow, identifying obstacles/roadblocks and working with team members to establish corrective actions that result in a successful and timely outcome. The MEGL develops processes to insure the integrity of engineering documentation (CAD models, drawings, safety analyses, data, etc.) The MEGL will maintain their technical expertise in a chosen area through individual technical contributions The MEGL position has onsite work requirements with the option for a hybrid arrangement Responsibilities: Personnel management: prepare, review, and conduct performance evaluations for the ME group, recruiting and retention, and conflict resolution Track and communicate progress: Conduct group meetings (design, assembly, production control); identity issues and blocks, review project status (design, fabrication, assembly, and integration), prepare a weekly project status report Team Engagement: Participate in design reviews and provide technical support in key project working group meetings. Technical work: Perform engineering work in an area of the individual’s expertise (design, analysis, project management, systems engineering, etc.) Fiscal management: Prepare and manage budget and support contracts (Tot. >$5M) Accountable for engineering documentation quality through ownership of standards, and approval and change management processes Other duties as assigned Required Bachelor’s degree in Mechanical Engineering, or for exceptional candidates a Bachelor’s degree in a STEM field with significant years of relevant experience Demonstrated experience managing a team of engineers, specialists, and / or technicians and 5+ years of engineering experience in design, analysis, build and/or testing. Management: Communication (verbal and written), delegation, empowerment, decision making, presentation skills, strategic thinking, change management, teamwork, consensus building, conflict resolution Candidate must have the ability to obtain a DOE Q clearance Preferred Bachelor degree in Mechanical Engineering (BSME) plus degree in Engineering Management or Business Administration 5+ years managing a multidisciplinary group (engineers, technicians, tool makers) responsible for the design, fabrication, assembly, and integration/assembly. Experience with scientific instrument development, structural hardware in research environment, high vacuum and cleanroom spaces, and/or complex opto-mechanical systems. Experience in a dynamic research environment delivering custom designed solutions and low volume production. Technical: Working knowledge of CAD, GD&T, Standards (ASME, ANSI), FEA, test and measurement, Product Lifecycle Management, Project management, and Enterprise-class engineering software APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $90,000 to $175,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191322": {
      "title": "Senior Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics The Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) of the University of Rochester has an opening for the Manager of the National Laser Users Facility (NLUF) Program and Coordinator for all external users of the Omega Laser Facility at LLE. The NLUF Manager works with the Department of Energy (DOE) -NNSA office, Omega Facility operations staff, and the Omega Experimental and Theory Divisions staff to ensure the success of experimental campaigns led by external users. In addition to the NLUF Program, the position coordinates the Laboratory Basic Sciences (LBS) Program that enables scientists from DOE's National Laboratories and LLE to conduct research programs on the Omega facilities. The NLUF and LBS Programs execute approximately 600 experiments per year on the LLE facilities. LaserNet is another DOE user program in which LLE participates.  The User Manager serves as the point of contact with all Department of Energy sponsors of user access. The successful applicant will also be expected to broaden the research portfolio of the NLUF and LBS Programs, to seek and encourage new users, and to work with the broad high energy density physics (HEDP) community to increase advocacy and sponsorship. In addition to these responsibilities, the successful applicant can participate in the ongoing research programs at LLE. While the exact fraction of the time spent in research will be flexible, it is expected to be in the range of no more than 25% averaged over the year. The NLUF Manager is part of the senior management team at LLE and will be expected to communicate the results of work carried out on the facility to NNSA and other sponsors, the national laboratories, and the broader HEDP and Inertial Confinement Fusion scientific community Specific NLUF responsibilities include: Conduct and coordinate with NNS the NLUF (bi-annual) solicitation for shot time on Omega, Conduct the annual Laboratory Basic Science (LBS) solicitation to NNSA and Office of Science laboratories Sub-committee chair for the Basic Science program on the Omega Facility Advisory and Scheduling Committee (FASC) User coordinator- communicate FASC decisions to all users (including LLE users), resolve conflicts Own and maintain the Omega Users Guidance Documents and Training Liaison to Omega Laser Users Group (OLUG) Communicate user community status to distinguished visitors, sponsor visitors, University Leadership and Trustees. LaserNet Point of Contact. Experimental User Coordinator functional tasks include the following: Form review committees to review and merit-rank the NLUF and LBS proposals. Coordinate LLE feasibility analysis for proposed NLUF and LBS experiments, Document committee discussions of NLUF proposal review for DOE including recommendations on proposal approval/disapproval and shot/funding allocation. Support the NNSA in communicating the results of the NLUF review to the principal investigators. Communicate results of LBS review to the principal investigators. Communicate results of NLUF and LBS reviews to LLE management. Solicit and edit annual summaries of all external user experiments for the LLE Annual Report. Solicit, maintain and communicate information on external user publication activity. Solicit, maintain and communicate information on external user graduate student research and employment destinations. Support, LLE, NNSA and federal sponsors on the continuous improvement of the programs. Advise on applications of AI & Machine Learning which will benefit the user community. In addition to these specific responsibilities, the successful applicant is to help develop new initiatives, grow the sponsor base and be an advocate for external users both within and outside of LLE. This includes: Outreach to Universities and Industry for NLUF Outreach to DOE laboratories for LBS Advise Leadership on future user program. Qualifications: Candidates must have a Ph.D. in a relevant area of high-energy-density physics or related field a demonstrated record of scientific accomplishment and leadership, and excellent communication and organizational skills Candidates should have prior experience as a user of the Omega facility,  equivalent experience in user facilities for HEDP research and engagement with academic institutions and national laboratories APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; four letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $140,000 to $215,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193276": {
      "title": "Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics POSITION SUMMARY The Laboratory for Laser Energetics (LLE) is seeking a Scientist in the High-Energy-Density Physics (HEDP) Experiments group to conduct innovative experimental research in HED material properties and/or atomic physics using advanced x-ray diagnostics. The successful candidate will design, lead, execute, and analyze experiments that use x-ray spectroscopy, scattering, and/or diffraction to study matter (e.g., condensed matter, warm-dense matter, dense plasmas) under extreme pressure and temperature. Research will address fundamental properties of HED matter that may include equation of state, phase transitions, electronic structure and band-gap behavior, opacity and related atomic, material, and transport properties. The candidate should have a strong record of experimental research and scientific publication, with demonstrated experience developing or applying x-ray diagnostics to dynamic-compression or other HED experiments. Extensive experience conducting experiments at high-power laser facilities and/or advanced light sources is highly desirable. Relevant facilities may include Omega, NIF, LCLS, APS, or the European XFEL. The successful candidate will work closely with experimentalists, engineers, and theorists/modelers to develop new measurement capabilities and establish a quantitative understanding of matter at extreme conditions. RESPONSIBILITIES Conceive, design, lead, and execute HEDP experiments using x-ray spectroscopy, scattering, diffraction, and/or related diagnostics to measure the material and/or atomic properties of matter at extreme conditions. Lead experimental campaigns at LLE and external high-power laser facilities or advanced light sources, including planning, shot execution, diagnostic configuration, data acquisition, and interpretation. Collaborate with target fabrication, laser, diagnostic, and engineering teams to develop and field experimental platforms and measurement capabilities. Work closely with theorists and computational scientists to design experiments, model diagnostic observables, interpret measurements, and connect experimental results to atomic and material models. Develop research proposals to secure experimental time at Omega and external facilities Publish results in peer-reviewed journals and present findings at national and international conferences and workshops. Mentor graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and other early-career scientists, as appropriate. QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Experimental Physics, Engineering, or a closely related field. At least 2 years of postdoctoral or equivalent research experience in experimental HEDP, shock physics, condensed matter physics, plasma physics, atomic physics, or a closely related field is preferred. Demonstrated record of experimental research excellence and peer-reviewed publication. Substantial experience leading experiments at high-power laser facilities and/or advanced light sources. Demonstrated expertise in one or more x-ray diagnostic techniques, particularly x-ray spectroscopy, scattering, or diffraction, to extract quantitative information about matter under extreme conditions. Experience working collaboratively with scientists, engineers, diagnostic specialists, and computational/modeling researchers. ESSENTIAL SKILLS Ability to independently lead complex experimental campaigns from scientific concept through experiment design, execution, data analysis, and publication. Strong experimental skills with x-ray emission or absorption spectroscopy, resonant or non-resonant x-ray scattering, x-ray diffraction, or related techniques. Strong understanding of HED matter and the physics underlying phase transitions, equation of state, electronic structure, ionization, and/or charge-state distributions. Experience leading experiments at high-power laser facilities and/or advanced light sources, including working effectively within large, multidisciplinary user-facility teams. Programming and scientific data-analysis experience, preferably using Python or comparable scientific computing tools. Excellent written and oral communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to publish and present scientific results. Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. The successful candidate will join the HEDP Experiments group as a Scientist or at a rank commensurate with experience. a cover letter; a curriculum vitae and a list of publications; at least two letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 Annually The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195133": {
      "title": "Scientist (Theoretical Studies of Laser-Material Interactions)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: LLE-Laboratory for Laser Energ Location: Laboratory for Laser Energetics Job Overview: Develop modeling capabilities to investigate the interaction of laser pulses with nanostructures and nanofeatures. Investigate the origin of laser induced damage in complex optical materials (gratings, multilayer dielectric coatings, meta optics, mixed materials) . Help develop designing criteria for next generation optical coatings with extended lifetime performance. Active communication/collaboration with other internal and external groups with targeted scope of work that is relevant to LLE’s current and future needs. Responsibilities and Duties: Work closely with scientist and engineers with expertise in optical design, coating fabrication, material characterization and laser damage testing. Interface with LLE staff while collaborating on projects and supporting facility operations. Communicate research results through internal reports, publish in refereed journals, and present at internal laboratory meetings and conference settings. Participate or lead research proposal grant seeking and writing Survey, study, and peer review relevant scientific literature Responsible for developing and maintaining computational capabilities in support of optical materials development and fabrication of coating and laser components Lead new projects and research within area of expertise as needs arise. Skills, Knowledge, and Abilities: PhD – Physics, Applied Physics, Materials Science, or other relevant fields At least 2 years from graduation of experience in research and development Strong publications record or equivalent experience Experience using multiphysics computational tools . Strong background in electromagnetic theory and applications. Research experience in the interaction of high intensity electromagnetic waves with material (propagation, energy coupling, nonlinear processes etc.) The applicant should submit: a cover letter a curriculum vitae and a list of publications at least three letters of recommendation SALARY RANGE: $100,000 to $140,000 The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates are set within the job's compensation range, and determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194449": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Electrical and Computer Engineering- 22HAJ-220542-ECE-FY27-1024, 1025, 1026",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Hajim School of Engineering & Applied Sciences: Electrical & Computer Engg Location: Computer Studies The University of Rochester’s Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering seeks to hire outstanding early career candidates in AI circuits and systems over the next three years. Appointment will be at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor. We seek outstanding candidates in three areas: Computer architecture for AI hardware. Accelerator architecture, hardware-software co-design, memory systems, and domain-specific architectures. Circuit design for AI hardware. Energy-efficient inference and training circuits, mixed-signal and in-memory computing, evolving beyond-CMOS technologies, and heterogeneous integration and advanced packaging. Edge AI and robotics. Embedded and real-time AI systems, autonomous systems, and human-AI interaction, with an emphasis on hardware-aware design. Successful candidates will have an anticipated start date between 2027 and 2029. The start date is negotiable. The University of Rochester is a distinguished research university, and the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering currently supports over $23M in active research grants, with an average research expenditure per research active faculty member of over $500K per year. The department is home to more than 65 full-time PhD students and a graduate program with over 20 MS students in residence at any given time, along with a BS program in Electrical and Computer Engineering. Successful candidates will have access to design, fabrication, characterization, and test infrastructure for integrated circuits and systems, high-performance computing resources, and collaboration opportunities through the Goergen Institute for Data Science and Artificial Intelligence and the Laboratory for Laser Energetics. Faculty also engage with the growing microelectronics corridor in upstate New York. The city of Rochester, NY is a diverse, energetic, and family-friendly community located on Lake Ontario adjacent to the Finger Lakes region with a metropolitan population of 1+ million people. Rochester also boasts a thriving arts and music culture, substantial employment opportunities in a variety of sectors, affordable housing, a rich history of social justice, and easy access to urban, suburban, and rural neighborhoods. Candidates should hold a PhD degree (or equivalent) in electrical and computer engineering, computer science, or a related field by the time their faculty appointment begins, and should have considerable research experience. Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter, 2) a CV, 3) a statement of research, 4) a teaching statement, 5) (optional) a statement discussing the ways in which your experiences will shape your pursuits as a member of our faculty and help you add to the University's core values of Meliora (ever better), 6) up to three articles (as three separate attachments), and 7) name of three people who can provide a letter of recommendation. Questions concerning this position can be addressed to Eby Friedman, eby.friedman@rochester.edu , chair of the search committee. Review of applications will begin on December 1 st , 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The referenced pay range represents the full base range of pay for this job. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on market data, experience and expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations. The University of Rochester community is committed to Meliora – becoming ever better. Through our Vision & Values statement ( https://www.rochester.edu/aboutus/values/ ) we affirm our commitment to equity, leadership, integrity, openness, respect and accountability."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170468": {
      "title": "Donna Strickland Optica Distinguished Professorship",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Hajim School of Engineering & Applied Sciences: The Institute of Optics Location: The Institute of Optics, University of Rochester The Institute of Optics at The University of Rochester seeks to hire an outstanding  candidate in the areas of optics, photonics and laser science. The appointment will be at the tenured Full Professor level with the title Donna Strickland Optica Distinguished Professorship. The University of Rochester is a distinguished research university, and the The Institute of Optics is the original academic program and research center in the United States to be entirely devoted to optical science and engineering. We offer undergraduate, master’s, and PhD degrees, have many active labs, affiliated research centers and work closely with Luminate, the largest optics-oriented business accelerator program in the nation. Faculty conduct research in a diverse range of optics-related fields, including biomedical optics, optical engineering, polarization, laser science, image science, nanophotonics,  optical materials, and quantum optics. There is excellent collaboration among faculty across areas, and close ties to other groups in the University, particularly the Center for Visual Science, Biomedical Engineering,  Physics, and the Laboratory for Laser Energetics. The city of Rochester, NY is a diverse, energetic, and family-friendly community located on Lake Ontario adjacent to the Finger Lakes region with a metropolitan population of 1 million. Rochester also boasts a thriving arts and music culture, substantial employment opportunities in various sectors, affordable housing, a rich history of social justice, and easy access to urban, suburban, and rural neighborhoods. Salary: $200,000 - $300,000 The referenced pay range represents the full base range of pay for this job. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on market data, experience, and expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations. The University of Rochester community is committed to Meliora – becoming ever better. Through our Vision & Values statement ( https://www.rochester.edu/aboutus/values/ ) we affirm our commitment to equity, leadership, integrity, openness, respect and accountability. The University of Rochester is an Equal Opportunity Employer with a strong commitment to diversity, and actively encourages applications from candidates from groups underrepresented in higher education. EOE / Minorities / Females / Protected Veterans / Disabled Successful candidates will show evidence of significant scholarly accomplishment and research productivity, excellent student mentoring, and high quality teaching. Candidates should hold a PhD degree (or equivalent) in optics, physics, electrical engineering or another related discipline, and should have considerable research experience. Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter, 2) a CV, 3) a statement of research, 4) a teaching statement, 5) a statement discussing the ways in which your experiences will shape your pursuits as a member of our faculty and help you add to the University's core values of Meliora (ever better), 6) up to three articles (as three separate attachments), and 7) contact information for three referees who we may contact at later stages of the interview process. Questions concerning this position can be addressed to Gary Wicks at gwicks@ur.rochester.edu . Review of applications will begin on September 15, 2025 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194378": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Full Professor in African History: 21SAS-210508-BLS-FY27-1002",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Black Studies Location: Rochester - NY The Department of Black Studies at the University of Rochester invites applications and nominations for a tenured faculty appointment at the rank of Advanced Associate Professor or Full Professor in African History , to begin July 1, 2027. We seek a distinguished scholar with an internationally recognized record of research excellence, impactful teaching, and visionary institutional leadership. Chronological period, geographical focus, and methodological approach within African history are open. We particularly encourage applications from scholars whose research connects continental African history with secondary specializations or comparative frameworks in Caribbean, Central American, South American, or Afro-European histories. Relevant areas of focus include, but are not limited to: Transatlantic circulations and diaspora studies Colonial and postcolonial entanglements Maritime trade and Indian Ocean/Atlantic networks Intellectual, political, and transnational networks The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active, high-impact research program, teach undergraduate survey courses and advanced seminars in African history, advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, direct doctoral dissertations in History, and provide strong leadership within the department and across interdisciplinary university initiatives, including working closely with related programs. Salary & Teaching Expectations Salary Range: $140,000 – $200,000 per year, commensurate with rank, qualifications, and experience. Teaching Load: Standard teaching load for research-active faculty in the department is 2/2 (four courses per academic year), alongside active mentorship and participation in departmental governance. Education: Ph.D. in Black/Africana Studies, History, or a closely related discipline. Rank & Research: A tenured profile with a sustained record of peer-reviewed publications (e.g., major monographs and influential journal articles) demonstrating exceptional scholarly impact and an ambitious ongoing research trajectory. For appointment as Advanced Associate Professor: A second book published or in press is required. For appointment as Full Professor: A record of distinguished scholarly achievement commensurate with senior rank. Teaching & Mentorship: Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate and graduate instruction, alongside a proven commitment to mentoring graduate students and directing doctoral dissertations. Service & Leadership: Evidence of significant departmental citizenship, collaborative interdisciplinary engagement, and a willingness to contribute to faculty development and curricular innovation. For full consideration, complete application packages must be submitted by November 1, 2026 . Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Applicants should upload the following materials: Cover letter Curriculum vitae (CV) Statement of teaching philosophy Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin immediately following the deadline and will continue until the position is filled. For additional inquiries, please contact the chair of the search committee, Stefanie K. Dunning, directly at Stefanie.Dunning@rochester.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192002": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Macroeconomics - 21SAS-210530-ECO- FY27-1004",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Economics Location: Rochester, NY The Department of Economics at the University of Rochester is recruiting one faculty member at the rank of assistant professor. The search is focused on applicants whose work and interests emphasize macroeconomics and/or international macro. The Department is looking for a high level of demonstrated scholarship, the potential to make important research contributions, and the ability to teach at undergraduate and graduate levels. Ph.D. in Economics Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and copies of (or links to) three relevant publications and/or writings. For final review, candidates will be asked to provide three letters of recommendation. The review process will begin on November 16, 2026; however, applications may still be submitted after this date."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193830": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Probability: 21SAS-210600-MTH- FY27-1008",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Mathematics Location: Hylan The Department of Mathematics at the University of Rochester invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in probability, broadly construed. We seek outstanding early-career candidates with a PhD in pure or applied mathematics, statistics, or a related field by the beginning of the appointment, and a record of excellence in research and teaching. This appointment will strengthen the department’s research in probability and support interdisciplinary collaborations aligned with the University’s Boundless Possibility strategic plan. We welcome candidates working across all areas of probability, with particular interest in applications and connections with other areas of mathematics, applied and theoretical sciences, and engineering. We especially encourage applications from candidates whose research connects with areas of current University strengths and planned growth. Opportunities include interactions with physics through statistical mechanics, complex systems, and random networks; biology through mathematical evolutionary genetics and stochastic models of biological systems; brain and cognitive sciences through probabilistic models of neural activity, learning, and cognition; computer science and data science through the mathematical foundations of artificial intelligence and machine learning; and economics, political science, and business through stochastic modeling, game theory, and decision making under uncertainty. These examples are not exhaustive. The successful candidate will be expected to develop a strong independent research program, contribute actively to the department’s research community, and pursue productive collaborations within the department and across the University. We also seek an enthusiastic teacher and mentor who will contribute to the department’s undergraduate and graduate programs, support students from a broad range of disciplines, and advance the University’s educational mission. The University of Rochester is a private, R1 research university located in upstate New York. The mathematics department has a tradition of strength in both research and teaching. Current research areas include mathematical physics, ergodic theory, probability, algebra and number theory, analysis, combinatorics, geometry, and topology. The department has a strong undergraduate program, offering BS and BA degrees in mathematics (both with research honors options), a BS in applied mathematics, and a joint BS degree in mathematics and statistics. There are also certificate programs in operations research and actuarial studies. Rochester is located on Lake Ontario and close to the Finger Lakes region, providing excellent living and recreational opportunities spanning all four seasons. The university is home to the world-famous Eastman School of Music and the Memorial Art Gallery, contributing to a community with a rich arts, music, and entertainment culture. Questions concerning this position can be addressed to Prof. Doug Ravenel, chair of the search committee, or Prof. Maria Gordina, chair of the Department of Mathematics. PhD in pure or applied mathematics, statistics, or a related field As part of your application, please submit the following: · Cover letter · Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications · A statement of research accomplishments and future plans · A statement of teaching experience and interests · Three representative publications · Four references, at least one of which addresses the applicant’s teaching. Consideration of applicants will begin November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194351": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Mathematics Location: Hylan The Department of Mathematics at the University of Rochester invites applications for several non-tenure track Visiting Assistant Professor positions for new or recent PhDs. The positions are for two years, renewable for a third upon satisfactory performance. Qualifications include a PhD in mathematics, strong evidence of excellence in the teaching of undergraduates, and outstanding promise in research. Successful applicants are expected to reside in the greater Rochester metro area during the term of their appointment. Research areas represented by the department, including algebraic topology, analysis, combinatorics, differential geometry and global analysis, number theory, and probability, will be preferred.  On your application, please indicate your primary area of study by filling out the Field of Study form.  Also, please indicate who you prefer to have as a  research mentor on the Faculty Mentor Form. Consideration of applicants will begin November 1, 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. For questions/concerns pertaining to the position, email Sema Salur at sema.salur@rochester.edu or Masha Gordina at mgordina@ur.rochester.edu . PhD in mathematics, strong evidence of excellence in the teaching of undergraduates, and outstanding promise in research. As part of your application, please submit the following: · Curriculum vitae · Research and teaching statements · Four letters of recommendation (with at least one providing evidence of teaching excellence · Student teaching evaluation scores and any other teaching awards or distinctions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193884": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Philosophy: 1SAS-210620-PHL- FY27-1010",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Philosophy Location: Rochester - NY The Department of Philosophy at the University of Rochester invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor. We welcome candidates with outstanding research potential who can work productively with colleagues and doctoral students in and beyond their area of specialization. We are looking for a researcher who works on normative questions about artificial intelligence, broadly construed. We will look closely at any research program examining ethical, social, political, or any other normative dimensions and consequences of any major aspect of artificial intelligence (including but not limited to safety, alignment, fairness, privacy, and economic impact).  Excellence in teaching at diverse levels of instruction is essential, and consideration will be given to candidates’ potential for collaboration with colleagues in other disciplines. The University of Rochester is a selective private R1 institution.  Course load: 2-2, plus doctoral supervision and normal departmental service. Applications received by November 1, 2026, will receive full consideration.  Direct inquiries to Philosophy Department Chair, Paul Audi . PhD by time of appointment. Candidates should submit a cover letter, writing sample, CV, and three confidential letters of recommendation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194428": {
      "title": "Joseph H. Eberly Endowed Professorship in physics-21SAS-210650-PAS-FY27-1011",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Bausch & Lomb The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Rochester invites applications for a full (tenured) professorship in theoretical quantum physics to hold the Joseph H. Eberly Endowed Professorship in physics. The University of Rochester has identified quantum science and technology as an area of current strength and future growth in its strategic plan.  Successful candidates will be expected to build on this tradition of excellence in quantum science across several departments at the University and to lead new initiatives, including through the University of Rochester Center for Coherence and Quantum Science. Tenure upon initial appointment for qualified candidates may be granted, contingent on favorable review of the candidate’s dossier by the department, the dean and the Provost. Complementing the professorship, the Joseph H. Eberly Endowed Professorship Research Fund will provide the professorship holder with substantial resources to pursue discovery—support that can be used to hire students or postdoctoral fellows, purchase specialized equipment, or seed early-stage, proof-of-concept projects. Applicants should have a Ph.D. in physics or a related field, an outstanding record of research, and a track record of excellence in teaching and mentorship at the graduate and undergraduate levels. Applications from candidates without a current full professorship will also be considered. Applications should include: • Cover letter • Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications • A statement of research accomplishments and future plans • A statement of teaching experience and interests • A list of five significant/representative publications • Three optional letters of recommendation Inquiries about the position should be directed to: Prof. John Nichol University of Rochester john.nichol@rochester.edu Applications will be reviewed beginning January 1, 2027 until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189376": {
      "title": "Political Science Adjunct-Fiandach",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Political Science Location: Harkness Position Summary The Department of Political Science at the University of Rochester invites applications for part-time Adjunct Faculty positions. Adjunct faculty teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in Political Science on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on departmental instructional needs and enrollment. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and student engagement while contributing to the Department's educational mission. Required · Master's degree in Political Science or a closely related field by the time of appointment. · Demonstrated subject matter expertise in the area(s) of instruction. · Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred · Ph.D. in Political Science or a closely related discipline. · Previous experience teaching at the college or university level. · Evidence of effective, inclusive teaching practices. · Experience using instructional technologies and learning management systems. PSCI-240"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192333": {
      "title": "Political Science Methodology Professor, TT, 21SAS-210580-PSC- FY27-1012",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Political Science Location: Rochester, NY The Department of Political Science at the University of Rochester invites applications for a tenure-track position in political methodology at the rank of assistant professor, beginning July 1, 2027. We welcome candidates who hold a PhD or are ABD in political science or a related discipline—including statistics, computer science, and economics—and whose methodological research addresses substantive questions in politics and political economy. We will consider all applications received by September 15, 2026, although we will continue to accept applications after that date. PhD or are ABD in political science or a related discipline Methodological research should addresses substantive questions in politics and political economy Candidates should submit the following documents: cover letter, CV, research statement, teaching statement, three confidential letters of recommendation, and a writing sample."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193309": {
      "title": "Instructional Track Program Director (Open Rank) - 21SAS-210530-ECO- FY27-1005",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 10, 2026 Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: School of Arts & Sciences: Political Science Location: Rochester - NY The University of Rochester’s Goergen Institute for Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (GIDS-AI), Department of Economics, and Department of Political Science invite applications for an instructional-track faculty position at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank to help launch, lead, and administer a new in-person MS in Quantitative Social Science degree program. This MS program is a new joint initiative of the three units aimed at providing rigorous training in statistical, computational, and data-analytic methods for addressing social science problems. We seek candidates with expertise in quantitative methods and their application to substantive problems in the social sciences. The anticipated start date for the position is July 1, 2027. Responsibilities: Develop and teach courses for master’s-level students Build relationships with external organizations and industry partners to develop capstone projects and supervise those projects Advise students in the MS program Manage the day-to-day administration of the MS program, including admissions and enrollment, curriculum development and oversight, marketing, and coordination with related administrative functions across the University. Applicants must have a PhD in political science, economics, statistics, computer science, data science, or a related field. Experience with graduate teaching and curriculum development is preferred. Preference will be given to candidates with experience applying quantitative methods to political science or economics through academic research, teaching, and/or industry experience. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, teaching statement, and the names and contact information for two references. Letters of recommendation are not required at the application stage, but may be requested at a later date. Questions concerning this position may be directed to Paulo Barelli , Professor and Chair of the Economics Department, Mujdat Cetin , Professor and Director of GIDS-AI, or David Primo , Professor and Chair of the Political Science Department. Applications received by December 15, 2026, will be given priority consideration. Applications received after that date will be considered until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191280": {
      "title": "Professor of Conducting and Ensembles & Director of Choral, Open Rank(tenure eligible)(30ESM-300910-CON-FY27-1013)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Eastman School of Music: Conducting & Ensembles Location: Eastman School Of Music The Eastman School of Music of the University of Rochester invites applications for a full-time faculty appointment in Choral Conducting beginning 1 July 2027. Rank and salary will be commensurate with experience. Responsibilities include leading choral ensembles, teaching graduate conducting students, teaching conducting classes, and maintaining national/international standing as a conductor and teacher. The faculty member will contribute to the musical life of the Eastman School of Music by participating in recruitment, auditions, juries, and concerts. In compliance with the NY Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000 - $170,000. The University of Rochester considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, and internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates should have a strong reputation as a conductor on the national and/or international level, in addition to a proven record of successful recruiting and teaching of outstanding students in a conservatory or university music school. Candidates should hold a masters degree or equivalent. For assisatance with the application please reach out to mailbox humanresources@esm.rochester.edu Review of applications will begin on September 22, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To receive full consideration, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, links to 3 recordings of performances and teaching examples (e.g. masterclasses, rehearsals, lessons), and three confidential letters of recommendation. Additional materials may be requested at a later date."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186705": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-ACC",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Simon Business School: Simon Grad Sch Faculty Location: Dewey Responsibilities: Under the direction of the Academic Department Chair or Dean: Develop and manage syllabus materials. The adjunct professor is responsible for selecting and compiling tests, assignments and/or online discussion exercises that permit measurement of performance relative to standardized learning objectives. Coordinate courseware and curriculum with academic department. The adjunct professor is responsible for reviewing any textbook and other courseware changes with the academic department chair and other full-time faculty teaching the course. Facilitate Class Instruction. The adjunct professor is responsible for teaching the assigned class in accordance with learning objectives and session plan outlined by the Simon Business School. Evaluate Student Performance. The adjunct professor is expected to administer evaluations of student performance based on course deliverables and course rubrics. All course grades will be posted in either Darden or the Blackboard Grade Center. Adhere to Protocol Guidelines. The adjunct professor is responsible for participation in any chat sessions as determined by the course. Respond Promptly to Grade Determination. The adjunct professor should submit grades for any assignments, discussion board exercises, and exams and course completion. Input Final Grades in online grading center."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192334": {
      "title": "Clinical/Executive Professor of Strategy and Consulting (Full-time)-23SIM-230720-SIM-FY27-1037",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Simon Business School: Simon Grad Sch Faculty Location: Dewey Simon Business School at the University of Rochester invites applications for an Executive or Clinical Professor (open rank) with substantial professional experience in management consulting to play a significant role in the School's MBA program. We seek an accomplished consulting professional who is excited about bringing the methods, disciplines, and perspectives of consulting practice into a leading business school environment. The successful candidate will have a substantial instructional role; develop and supervise consulting-oriented experiential projects with industry partners; and coach students pursuing consulting careers. The position is particularly well suited to an experienced consulting professional interested in applying the knowledge, judgment, and relationships developed over a successful industry career to teaching, mentoring, and developing the next generation of business leaders. Key Responsibilities Teaching and Curriculum Development The faculty member will have a substantial instructional role in the MBA program and may also contribute to other Simon graduate and undergraduate programs. Teaching responsibilities are expected to include courses and learning experiences in areas such as: Structured problem solving , including framing ambiguous business problems, developing hypotheses, prioritizing analyses, synthesizing evidence, and translating analysis into recommendations; Consulting methods and problem-solving approaches used by leading strategy and management consulting organizations; Business process analysis, design, and improvement , including diagnosing processes and identifying improvement opportunities; Executive communication and recommendation development , including communicating complex analyses clearly and persuasively; and Related areas drawing on the faculty member's professional expertise. They will provide high-quality in-person, synchronous online, and asynchronous instruction and will develop and manage course materials, assessments, and related activities in accordance with applicable University and School policies. Experiential Learning and Industry Projects A key component of the role will be expanding opportunities for Simon students to work on substantive consulting-style projects with external organizations. The faculty member will: Help develop relationships with companies, consulting firms, alumni, nonprofit organizations, and other partners that can provide high-quality experiential projects; Work with organizational sponsors to scope meaningful projects that provide strong learning opportunities for students; Supervise and coach student consulting teams throughout their engagements; and Help students develop the professional disciplines associated with high-quality consulting, including problem definition, analytical rigor, project management, client communication, teamwork, and synthesis; Student Career Advising and Coaching Working in partnership with Simon's career services team and other faculty and staff, the faculty member will serve as an important resource for students pursuing consulting careers. Responsibilities will include: Advising students on careers in strategy, management, specialized, internal, and related consulting and advisory roles; Coaching students on consulting recruiting, including case and behavioral interviews, networking, and professional positioning; Mentoring students as they evaluate consulting opportunities and prepare for internships and full-time positions; and Contributing to workshops, case-preparation activities, speaker programs, employer engagement, and other consulting-related career-development initiatives. Compensation In compliance with the NY Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $130,000 - $260,000. The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries will be set within the job’s compensation range, and will be determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations. About Simon Business School Simon Business School at the University of Rochester is known for an analytically rigorous approach to management education. Simon's programs combine strong foundations in economics and analytics with experiential learning and leadership development, preparing students to turn rigorous analysis and insight into meaningful business impact. The University of Rochester is a leading private research university offering the intellectual resources of a major research institution together with a highly collaborative academic community. This position offers an experienced consulting leader the opportunity to bring the best of professional practice into that environment— teaching and mentoring talented students, creating applied learning experiences, engaging with industry, and helping shape an important area of the school's future development. Candidates should bring substantial professional experience in management consulting or closely related advisory work, including significant responsibility for solving complex client problems, leading engagements or teams, and working with senior executives. Required qualifications include: Graduate degree in business or related field Significant professional experience in management consulting or closely related advisory work; Interest and ability to teach MBA, MS, and Undergraduate students in problem solving, consulting, and related areas of the Simon School curriculum. Previous university-level teaching experience is desirable but not required for candidates who demonstrate exceptional communication, facilitation, mentoring, and professional-development capabilities. Deep scholarly research credentials are not a central criterion for this role . We seek a practitioner-faculty member whose professional accomplishments, expertise, judgment, and ability to develop students will materially enhance Simon's consulting-related curriculum, experiential learning opportunities, and student outcomes."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193251": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor in Human Development (24WAR-240320-WAR-FY27-1039)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Warner School of Education and Human Development: Warner School of Education Faculty Location: Lechase Hall The Warner Graduate School of Education and Human Development at the University of Rochester invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor in Human Development . This position combines teaching, student mentorship, program development and improvement, and community-engaged partnership development. The term of employment will begin on July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will play a central role in advancing the Warner School's mission of leveraging the power of education to transform lives and make the world more just and humane. The Warner School’s Human Development program prepares graduates to understand and address complex issues affecting human development across educational, healthcare, organizational, and community contexts. The program emphasizes interdisciplinary inquiry, applied learning, and community engagement to foster healthy development across the lifespan. The program offers PhD, EdD, and master’s degrees. The person occupying this position would have primary responsibility for the master’s degree and field experiences. The position is designed for an innovative educator and community-engaged professional who can connect graduate education in human development with meaningful experiences and partnerships across the Greater Rochester region and beyond. We welcome applicants with doctorates in Human Development or related fields, including psychology, sociology, social work, or family studies. The applicant must have an educational and practical background that prepares them to teach graduate-level courses in human development and work with students and community organizations to design and facilitate varied opportunities for graduate students outside of the classroom. The principal duties are associated with this position are to: Teach the equivalent of six graduate courses annually in the Human Development program. Contribute to curriculum development and continuous program improvement. Design and oversee community-based learning opportunities that integrate academic coursework with practice-based experiences aligned with students' professional interests and career aspirations. Collaborate with other faculty in creating community-based research experiences for students Support students in identifying professional goals, navigating career pathways, and connecting academic learning to community-based practice. Supervise independent studies, capstone projects, internships, and other applied learning experiences as appropriate. Ideal candidates for this position will have qualifications and experiences that include: Doctorate in Human Development or related fields, earned prior to the position’s starting date. Experience and a demonstrated commitment to teaching and mentoring graduate students Evidence of successful community-engaged work, partnership development, or applied professional practice. A demonstratedcommitment topreparingeffective,justice-orientedprofessionals and researchers. Strong interpersonal, organizational, and collaborative skills. Preferred Qualifications Experience developing and sustaining partnerships within the Greater Rochester region, the Finger Lakes region, or comparable community ecosystems, with demonstrated understanding of local educational, health, human service, nonprofit, and community organizations. Established professional networks within Rochester-area community agencies, school systems, healthcare organizations, foundations, or civic institutions. Experience coordinating internships, practica, community-engaged learning, or workforce development initiatives. Expertise in one or more areas aligned with Human Development program strengths, including youth development, family studies, developmental differences, community development, or community-engaged scholarship. Experience working across university-community boundaries to develop mutually beneficial partnerships. The review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For more information about this position, please contact the search chair, Martin Lynch at mlynch@warner.rochester.edu . As part of the application, the following documentation will be required: A cover letter that addresses your interests in and qualifications for the position, including your research interests and priorities as well as your teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness CV Writing sample Contact information for three references About the Warner School: The Warner Graduate School of Education and Human Development is part of the University of Rochester, one of the world’s leading research-intensive universities. At the Warner School, we believe that education can transform lives and make the world more just and humane. This vision informs our teaching, research, and service as a research school of education, as we strive to: PREPARE practitioners and researchers who are knowledgeable, reflective, skilled, and caring educators, who can make a difference in individual lives as well as their fields, and who are leaders and agents of change; GENERATE and disseminate knowledge leading to new understandings of education and human development, on which more effective educational policies and practices can be grounded; and COLLABORATE across disciplines, professions, and constituencies to promote change that can significantly improve education and support positive human development. Our diverse work in each of these domains is informed by the following underlying beliefs: the improvement of education is in pursuit of social justice; development and learning shape and are shaped by the contexts in which they occur; the complexity of educational problems requires an interdisciplinary and collaborative approach; and wise practices are grounded in research and theory, just as useful theory and research are informed by practice. The Warner School fosters a learning community that represents and builds on the rich diversity of human experiences, backgrounds, cultures, histories, ideas, and ways of living. Learn more about equity, diversity, and inclusion at the Warner School here: https://www.warner.rochester.edu/about/diversity About Rochester: Located in western New York, Rochester is a vibrant city with a welcoming community, affordable housing, and rich intellectual and cultural resources. Approximately six hours by car from New York City and three hours from Toronto, our medium-sized city on the shore of Lake Ontario is a dynamic town, with many great restaurants, museums, businesses, and educational institutions. Discover what Rochester has to offer in all four seasons . Claiming more sites on the National Register of Historic Places than any other city its size, Rochester was also the home of women’s rights activist Susan B. Anthony and abolitionist Frederick Douglass. Their commitments to equity and justice are part of the DNA of the Rochester community and the University of Rochester. Visit our website to learn more about the University of Rochester ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193252": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor in Mental Health Counseling (24WAR-240320-WAR-FY27-1040)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Warner School of Education and Human Development: Warner School of Education Faculty Location: Lechase Hall The Warner Graduate School of Education and Human Development at the University of Rochester invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor in Mental Health Counseling . We are seeking a candidate who excels at teaching, both in-person and online, and clinical supervision, in ways that promote culturally sustaining practices. The term of employment will begin on July 1, 2027. The Clinical Mental Health Counseling program, which is part of the Department of Counseling and Human Development, is a 60-credit hour CACREP-accredited program. The program faculty seek to train ethical and competent professional counselors to diagnose and treat a variety of mental and emotional concerns and disorders. Our students can work with diverse clients, providing individual, couple, family, and group counseling in various settings. The program is designed to prepare graduates to work effectively as counselors offering services to people challenged by emotional, behavioral, and relationship issues. Well-grounded in counseling theory, research, and practice, our program prepares qualified, diversity-sensitive, license-eligible mental health counselors for practice. The program faculty are productive, nationally and internationally recognized counselors, teachers, and scholars who are also committed to improving the quality of mental health counseling and the development of the counseling field. The department prepares scientific practitioners with diverse career and research interests and backgrounds. We have developed several online programs and courses at Warner as part of our commitment to enhancing learning opportunities for students and ensuring equitable access. The program, as well as the Warner School, is dedicated to producing and using research to enhance the effectiveness of mental health counseling processes and practices. The Bridge program is an advanced certification pathway that allows Professional School Counselors to obtain LMHC licensure or Licensed Professional Counselors to obtain certification as a Professional School Counselor in New York State. This program offers hybrid and distance courses and is not CACREP-accredited. The principal duties associated with this position are to: Teach the equivalent of six graduate-level counseling courses using rigorous pedagogy that adheres to CACREP standards. Lead program recruitment efforts and interview program applicants. Provide program and career advising for master’s and doctoral candidates. Seek external funding to support program development, student success, and scholarship. Provide service/leadership to the department, Warner School, and Counseling profession. Collaborate with departmental and University partners to help build and sustain a thriving Counseling Center, help establish clinical operations, supervision structures, and community partnerships to support the Center's long-term success. Ideal candidates for this position will have qualifications and experiences that include: A Doctorate in Counselor Education & Supervision from a CACREP-accredited program with a specialization in REBT/CBT, Systems Theories, and/or Trauma-informed care (e.g., EMDR, TF-CBT, etc.). The doctoral degree must be earned before the position’s starting date. A master’s degree in Clinical Mental Health Counseling from a CACREP-accredited program. A commitment to the preparation of strong mental health counselors, leaders, and scholars at both the master’s and doctoral levels. Demonstrated ability to utilize competency-driven pedagogy (e.g., experiential learning, case-based interventions, simulated learning environments, etc.) to facilitate students’ clinical and cultural competencies. A commitment to The potential to complement the expertise of our current faculty in counseling and human development. Eligible to be licensed as an LMHC-D counselor with New York State, with a clear professional identity as a Professional Counselor. Demonstrated commitment to strengthening educational resources, opportunities, and trajectories for diverse, historically marginalized students, families, and communities. The ability to use technology tools flexibly and inventively to create powerful synchronous and asynchronous learning experiences, both in-person and online. Experience providing clinical supervision and overseeing clinical practice or agency setting. The mission of the Counseling Program at the Warner School of Education and Human Development is to educate counselors who will empower their clients and students to create more rewarding and meaningful lives and relationships; become more self-reflective, caring, and compassionate people; and contribute to reform of the systems in which their lives are embedded and to prepare counselor educators, researchers, practitioners, and leaders in the counseling profession who will educate the next generation of counselors to do all of the above; will contribute new knowledge in the fields of counseling, counselor education, and human development; and will be leaders in reforming educational and social systems to promote social justice, healthy human development, and quality relationships. This mission is based on an ecological perspective of professional counseling that acknowledges the importance of personal development but also recognizes that individual lives are embedded in a variety of personal, extended relationships, and social systems. The review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For more information about this position, please contact the search committee chair, Dr. Marissa Davala, at mdavala@warner.rochester.edu . As part of the application, the following documentation will be required: A cover letter that addresses your interests in and qualifications for the position, including your research interests and priorities as well as your teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness CV Writing sample Contact information for three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193253": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor in K-12 Professional Learning Education (24WAR-240320-WAR-FY27-1038)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: Academic Center: Warner School of Education and Human Development: Warner School of Education Faculty Location: Lechase Hall The Center for Professional Development and Education Reform in the Warner Graduate School of Education and Human Development at the University of Rochester invites applications for the position of Clinical Assistant Professor of Education . The term of employment will begin on July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will play a pivotal role in bridging research and practice in K-12 education, with a particular focus on STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics) professional learning. As a key leader in the Center, the faculty member will design, implement, and study innovative professional learning initiatives for K-12 educators. The role also includes leading major grant- and contract-funded projects that advance K-12 teaching and learning. The principal duties associated with this position are to: Engage in the development and delivery of high-quality professional learning programs for K-12 educators. Translate research into practice by designing and implementing evidence-based professional learning initiatives, with a focus on STEM education. Lead and manage major grant- and contract-funded projects, including proposal development, project execution, and reporting. Collaborate with school districts, community organizations, and other stakeholders to identify and address professional learning needs. Contribute to the Warner School’s mission by teaching courses, advising students, and engaging in service activities as appropriate. Disseminate findings and best practices through publications, presentations, and other professional outlets. Ideal candidates for this position will have qualifications and experiences that include: A doctoral degree in a relevant education or STEM discipline with a strong education research background. Proven success in securing external grants and contracts to support professional learning programs or research In-depth fluency with ongoing research in K-12 professional learning, demonstrating a deep understanding of the existing literature, methodologies, and current needs, particularly in STEM. Demonstrated ability to disseminate scholarly work. Exceptional communication and interpersonal abilities. Experience as a K-12 STEM teacher or higher education faculty member preferred The Center for Professional Development and Education Reform is dedicated to improving K-12 education through innovative professional learning, research, and partnerships. The Center’s integrated placement within a research school of education uniquely positions it to disseminate cutting-edge resources and ideas to the field, while also facilitating the return of challenges and questions from practitioners to the University for research purposes. The review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For more information about this position, please contact Michael Daley, Director of the Center for Professional Development and Education Reform, mdaley@warner.rochester.edu . As part of the application, the following documentation will be required: A cover letter that addresses your interests in and qualifications for the position, including your research interests and priorities as well as your teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness CV Writing sample Contact information for three references About the Warner School: The Warner Graduate School of Education and Human Development is part of the University of Rochester, one of the world’s leading research-intensive universities. At the Warner School, we believe that education can transform lives and make the world more just and humane. This vision informs our teaching, research, and service as a research school of education, as we strive to: PREPARE practitioners and researchers who are knowledgeable, reflective, skilled, and caring educators, who can make a difference in individual lives as well as their fields, and who are leaders and agents of change; GENERATE and disseminate knowledge leading to new understandings of education and human development, on which more effective educational policies and practices can be grounded; and COLLABORATE across disciplines, professions, and constituencies to promote change that can significantly improve education and support positive human development. Our diverse work in each of these domains is informed by the following underlying beliefs: the improvement of education is in pursuit of social justice; development and learning shape and are shaped by the contexts in which they occur; the complexity of educational problems requires an interdisciplinary and collaborative approach; and wise practices are grounded in research and theory, just as useful theory and research are informed by practice. The Warner School fosters a learning community that represents and builds on the rich diversity of human experiences, backgrounds, cultures, histories, ideas, and ways of living. Learn more about equity, diversity, and inclusion at the Warner School here: https://www.warner.rochester.edu/about/diversity About Rochester: Located in western New York, Rochester is a vibrant city with a welcoming community, affordable housing, and rich intellectual and cultural resources. Approximately six hours by car from New York City and three hours from Toronto, our medium-sized city on the shore of Lake Ontario is a dynamic town, with many great restaurants, museums, businesses, and educational institutions. Discover what Rochester has to offer in all four seasons . Claiming more sites on the National Register of Historic Places than any other city its size, Rochester was also the home of women’s rights activist Susan B. Anthony and abolitionist Frederick Douglass. Their commitments to equity and justice are part of the DNA of the Rochester community and the University of Rochester. Visit our website to learn more about the University of Rochester ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193214": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Surgery / Associate Professor of Surgery (Acute Care Surgery)",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: University of Rochester Medical Center: School of Medicine and Dentistry: Surgery: Surgery: Acute Care Location: School of Medicine and Dentistry The Department of Surgery at the University of Rochester and Strong Memorial Hospital, located in Rochester New York, is seeking a surgeon-scientist to join the Division of Trauma and Acute Care Surgery.  The successful applicant will join nine fellowship-trained, board-certified surgeons in the practice of Traumatology, Surgical Critical Care and Emergency General Surgery. We are seeking a surgeon-scientist with interest and expertise in rigorous scholarship in the field of acute care and trauma surgery and/or critical care.  Research focus may include basic, translational, clinical or other areas where academic pursuit will lead to improvements in the treatment of surgically-relevant disease. Opportunities for clinical and scientific collaboration are plentiful . Candidates would have departmental and divisional support, including research-focused time and infrastructure. Strong Memorial Hospital is a 940-bed, tertiary care referral center serving Rochester and the surrounding Finger Lakes Region of New York State. As the primary teaching hospital for the University of Rochester School of Medicine, Strong Memorial Hospital offers care in 40 different specialties and is ranked as one of \"America's Best Hospitals\" by U.S. News & World Report. The ACS verified Level I Trauma Center provides care for >3000 injured patients annually. The Finger Lakes Region is a beautiful place to live and raise a family, offering year-round activities for every season. Vibrant food and music venues, multiple outdoor recreational options, easy access to minor league sporting events and driving distance to most major Northeast Metropolitan areas. The greater Rochester area boasts some of the best public schools in the country. Candidates must have completed medical school receiving an MD or equivalent and completed a full surgical training residency program. Candidates must be BE/BC and must be eligible for faculty appointment at the assistant or associate professor level. Must be eligible for a full NYS medical license. If you already have an Interfolio account, please sign in to apply to this position.  If not, please create an Interfolio account.  For questions/concerns pertaining to the position, please email jodi_barker@urmc.rochester.edu or anne_hynes@urmc.rochester.edu The referenced pay range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the base range of compensation for this faculty position. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on (but not limited to) market data, experience and expertise of the individual, and with consideration to related position salaries. Alignment of clinical incentive-based compensation may also be applicable and will be discussed during the hiring process"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186811": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: University of Rochester Medical Center: School of Nursing and Health Sciences: SON Senior Leadership Location: Helen Wood Hall The University of Rochester School of Nursing seeks a full-time postdoctoral research associate to serve as Project Director for an NIH-funded study designed to develop and pilot test a novel couples-based addiction treatment intervention for heterosexual couples in which both partners have drug use disorders. The study uses formative mixed-methods research, intervention mapping, and stakeholder co-creation to develop a feasible, relationship-centered treatment model. Under the supervision of Dr. James McMahon, the postdoctoral associate will support study design, implementation, and dissemination. Responsibilities include developing qualitative and quantitative research tools; supporting recruitment, enrollment, and data collection with couples, providers, and administrators; managing Rochester-based field activities; contribute to data management, analysis, and mixed-methods integration; assist with intervention development, adaptation, training materials, and pre-pilot testing; support participant follow-up and retention; and contribute to manuscripts and presentations. This is a 2-year, full-time position with a negotiable start date. This position will be in person, and the successful candidate must reside in the Rochester, New York area for the duration of the postdoctoral appointment. Qualifications: Doctoral degree relevant field required by the time of appointment; strong research training in qualitative, quantitative, or mixed-methods approaches; excellent organizational and communication skills; and the ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multi-site research team. Research experience in the areas of substance use disorders, mental health, or vulnerable populations preferred. The referenced pay range represents the minimum and maximum compensation for this job. Individual annual salaries/hourly rates will be set within the job’s compensation range, and will be determined by considering factors including, but not limited to, market data, education, experience, qualifications, expertise of the individual, and internal equity considerations.”"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/141193": {
      "title": "Professor of Clinical; Associate Professor of Clinical; Assistant Professor of Clinical",
      "description": "Department: University of Rochester: Office of the Provost: University of Rochester Medical Center: School of Medicine and Dentistry: Anesthesiology & Perioperative Medicine Location: School of Medicine & Dentistry The Department of Anesthesiology and Perioperative Medicine is seeking a general anesthesiologist at the Professor of Clinical; Associate Professor of Clinical; Assistant Professor of Clinical level. Duties will include delivery of anesthesia care to patients, clinical and didactic teaching for residents, and supervision of CRNAs. The referenced pay range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the base range of compensation for this faculty position. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on (but not limited to) market data, experience and expertise of the individual, and with consideration to related position salaries. Alignment of clinical incentive-based compensation may also be applicable and will be discussed during the hiring process. Candidates should be board certified/eligible in Anesthesiology, possess an M.D. degree or equivalent, and possess the credentials to obtain an unrestricted NYS Medical license and DEA. If you already have an Interfolio account, please sign in to apply to this position. If not, please create an Interfolio account. The referenced pay range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the base range of compensation for this faculty position. Individual salaries will be determined within the job’s salary range and established based on (but not limited to) market data, experience and expertise of the individual, and with consideration to related position salaries. Alignment of clinical incentive-based compensation may also be applicable and will be discussed during the hiring process."
